bitpacking_ordered: batched reads

Preparation for #10010.

`bitpacking_ordered` is used to store file offsets in the HNSW graph file.
This PR adapts `bitpacking_ordered::Reader` so it can be both used in in-memory HNSW and UIO.

Before this PR we had this `bitpacking_ordered::Reader`:
```rust
// Holds `compressed: &'a [u8]` and compression parameters.
struct Reader<'a> { … }

impl Reader {
    // Get single value.
    // Uses unsafe inside to avoid excessive boundary checks.
    fn get(self, &index) -> usize;
}
```

After this PR:
```rust
// Holds only compression parameters, agnostic to the storage.
struct Reader { … }

// Behaves like the old `Reader`.
struct SliceReader<'a>(Reader, &'a [u8]);

impl SliceReader {
    // Used instead of old Reader::get().
    // Now returns a pair of values. No unsafe.
    fn read_pair(self, &index) -> Range<usize>;
}

impl Reader {
    pub fn read_pairs_iter(
        &self,
        s: &impl UniversalRead,
        indices: &[usize],
    ) -> UioResult<impl Iterator<…>>;
}
```

Notes:
- Removing `unsafe` from `Reader::get()` costs us ~60% of its performance on `bitpacking` micro-benchmarks.
- New interface returns pairs, not single values. (`get` -> `read_pair`)
  When using it for file offsets, we want to read pairs anyway.
  Specialized `read_pair` instead of two calls to `get()` reclaims a bit of performance back (so, only ~30% slower).
- But given that `bitpacking_ordered` is not the hottest part of HNSW search, the observable slowdown is ~0.5% in the worst case (on vector-db-benchmark).
- OTOH, after removing `unsafe`, the code can be less paranoid about safety invariants (search for `safety` or `#[deny` in the old code).
- `SliceReader` is integrated into HNSW search. The UIO version is not used yet.

Huge disk overhead with `custom` sharding approach

I was trying out Qdrant in cluster mode with custom sharding approach. I have just one node in the cluster. When I created an empty collection `test`, it did not take any memory or storage.

```
PUT collections/test
{
  "vectors": {
    "size": 3,
    "distance": "Cosine"
  },
  "sharding_method": "custom"
}
```
<img width="840" height="480" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/79065de4-433f-4955-a050-2286d4ac438a" />

But when I created a shard key, it started consuming a large chunk of storage (`~68 MB`). The memory consumption was still significantly low ( `< 10 KB`).

<img width="840" height="480" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6c8c3279-3d99-470b-b532-deee3df74801" />

Again, I inserted a small vector, and the disk storage increased by `30 MB`. The memory consumption was still significantly low.

```
PUT collections/test/points
{
  "shard_key": "1",
  "points": [
    {
      "id": 9,
      "payload": {
        "color": "red"
      },
      "vector": [
        0.1,
        0.1,
        0.8
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```
<img width="840" height="560" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c80e61d9-606d-4b13-be05-1274805423a0" />

I continued creating another shard  key, and again the storage consumption increased by about `60 MB`, and inserting a new point again increased the disk consumption by another `30 MB`. In the end of this exercise, I kept 2 points, each in one shard. The disk usage was `~200 MB`, but memory usage was just `~50 KB`.

I want to understand why creating a shard key or inserting a small vector adds up so much disk, while the memory usage still remains low. (It is understandable that metadata + index headers will consume some storage, but the figures are significantly large compared to the actual data size).

